            <div xml:id="d324">

                <pb facs="0433" n="338" xml:id="pg338"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8219: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dickover</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-10-04T13:00:00+09:00">October 4, 1936—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 4—4:19 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>204. 1. Press gives wide publicity to an interview yesterday between the Minister
                    for Foreign Affairs and Chinese Ambassador. Arita is said to have emphasized the
                    importance of the present negotiations for adjusting Sino-Japanese relations and
                    to have expressed the conviction that Japan’s true intentions would become known
                    when Kawagoe meets Chiang Kai Shek.</p>
                <p>2. There has been no recent allusion in press to any interview on Sino-Japanese
                    relations by the Foreign Office with any other diplomatic representative.</p>
                <p>3. Press states that Prime Minister left yesterday for Hokkaido to report
                    developments in Sino-Japanese situation to the Throne.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dickover</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
